Customer Support Database – Environment Migration Audit

This document summarizes code-level and configuration dependencies that can cause problems when copying the Customer Support Database application to another company’s systems.

Scope
- Project: WindowsApplication1 (main project).
- Excludes: backup folders like "WindowsApplication1 - Copy" and "WindowsApplication1 - org after restore".
- Focus: items that break or misbehave when server names, databases, networks, or email/report infrastructure differ.

1. Databases / SQL Server

1.1 App.config connection strings

File: WindowsApplication1\App.config

Current connectionStrings and userSettings examples:

  <connectionStrings>
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.LocalDB"
         connectionString="Data Source=LocalHost\SQLEXPRESS;Initial Catalog=CSDBLocal;Integrated Security=True;Encrypt=False;TrustServerCertificate=True" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString"
         connectionString="Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Sheppee_LiveConnectionString"
         connectionString="Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g" />
    <add name="CSDB.My.MySettings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString1"
         connectionString="Data Source=sheppee-sap-db;Initial Catalog=Customer_Support_DB;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g;Encrypt=False;TrustServerCertificate=True" />
  </connectionStrings>

  <userSettings>
    <CSDB.My.MySettings>
      <setting name="CSDBLocalConnectionString">
        <value>Data Source=localhost\sqlexpress;Initial Catalog=CSDBLocal;Integrated Security=True</value>
      </setting>
      <setting name="CSDBLocalConnectionStringTest">
        <value>Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g</value>
      </setting>
      <setting name="Sheppee_LiveConnectionStringTest">
        <value>Data Source=SHEPPEE-SAP-DB;Initial Catalog=Sheppee_Live;Persist Security Info=True;User ID=CSDB;Password=Z4k15MyD0g</value>
      </setting>
    </CSDB.My.MySettings>
  </userSettings>

Why this breaks elsewhere
- Other companies will not have:
  - LocalHost\\SQLEXPRESS with a CSDBLocal database.
  - A server called SHEPPEE-SAP-DB / sheppee-sap-db.
  - Databases named Customer_Support_DB and Sheppee_Live.
  - The CSDB SQL login and password.

What to do when migrating
- Replace all server names, database names, and credentials with the new company’s values.
- Decide per installation whether to use SQL authentication (User ID/Password) or Windows authentication (Integrated Security=True).
- Ensure CSDBLocalConnectionString points to the SQL instance where CSDBLocal is hosted (can be local or shared, not necessarily SQLEXPRESS).

1.2 Local helper DB CSDBLocal (ConnectionInfo, Stackers, Transfers)

File: WindowsApplication1\Class\ClsLocalDatabase.vb

Key members
- Base configuration (already updated in code):
  - LocalSQLConnectionString As String = My.Settings.CSDBLocalConnectionString
  - LocalDatabaseName As String = "CSDBLocal"
- Existence / creation helpers:
  - DoesSQLExist()
  - DoesDatabaseExist()
  - CreateDatabaseAndTable() – creates tables:
    - ConnectionInfo
    - Stackers
    - Transfers
- Seed data methods:
  - InsertDataIntoConnectionInfo() – seeds ConnectionInfo with CSDB and SAP rows (Sheppee defaults).
  - InsertDataIntoTransfers() – seeds Transfers with machine-specific transfer definitions (HS, VFT, etc.).
  - InsertDataIntoStackers() – seeds Stackers (Tri-Flex, MRS, Speed Liner, etc.).
- Environment/program connection string helpers:
  - TestSettingsConnectionStringCSDB()
  - TestSettingsConnectionStringSAP()
  - UpdateSAPConnectionString()
  - UpdateCSDBConnectionString()
  - GetconnectionStringFromProgramSettings(ConnectionDatabase As String)
  - TestConnectionStringSAP()
  - TestConnectionStringCSDB()
  - UpdateSettingsFromLocalConnectionInfo(sqlName As String)
  - ApplyConnectionInfoFromRow(...)

Risks for a new system
- Code is now environment-agnostic: all helper-DB logic uses LocalSQLConnectionString from My.Settings (no hard-coded server/instance).
- Remaining risks are configuration/data only:
  - If CSDBLocalConnectionString is not updated in App.config, all local DB operations still point at the default CSDBLocal location.
  - InsertDataIntoConnectionInfo seeds ConnectionInfo with Sheppee’s CSDB and SAP server details and encrypted credentials. These are valid defaults for the original environment only.
  - If a new company does not configure its own ConnectionInfo entries via FrmEnvironmentConnections, the application might still rely on these seeded values and attempt to connect to Sheppee servers.
  - TestSettingsConnectionStringCSDB and TestSettingsConnectionStringSAP use My.Settings.Customer_Support_DBConnectionString and My.Settings.Sheppee_LiveConnectionStringTest. If those settings are not updated, connection tests will fail.

What to do on a new system
- Configure My.Settings.CSDBLocalConnectionString in App.config to the new helper DB location (SQL instance + CSDBLocal database).
- Run the CSDBLocal creation and seed logic once on the chosen SQL instance (CreateDatabaseAndTable, InsertDataIntoConnectionInfo, InsertDataIntoTransfers, InsertDataIntoStackers).
- Use FrmEnvironmentConnections to create/update ConnectionInfo records for each environment:
  - {Environment}_CSDB
  - {Environment}_SAP
- Ensure ModEnvironment.ActiveEnvironmentName matches the environment names used when seeding ConnectionInfo.

2. File server / attachments

Where configured
- UI: WindowsApplication1\Forms\Admin\FrmAdminGeneral.Designer.vb
  - txtSaveAttachmentAddy – Root directory for saving attachments.
  - txtGoodsReturnsFolder – Optional override for Goods Returns attachments.
  - txtCustomerSupportLogFolder – Optional override for Customer Support Log.
  - txtCustomerSupportPoolFolder – Optional override for Customer Support Pool.

- Storage model: WindowsApplication1\Class\Program Objects\clsProgramSettings.vb
  - ServerDirectory As String
  - GoodsReturnsDirectory As String
  - CustomerSupportLogDirectory As String
  - CustomerSupportPoolDirectory As String
  - WarehouseID As Integer (environment specific, not a path).

- Logic: WindowsApplication1\Modules\ModFileManagment.vb
  - GetAttachmentRoot(directoryType As String)
    - Uses specific override directories if set.
    - Falls back to ServerDirectory + subfolder name.
  - CheckFileLocation(Directory_Type, Number)
    - Resolves the base folder using GetAttachmentRoot and creates subfolders per number.
  - Save_CSLFile and similar subs use these helpers.

Risks for a new system
- The attachment code path is now generic and driven entirely from GenSettings (no hard-coded server paths).
- Remaining risk is configuration only: ServerDirectory and overrides may point at paths that do not exist on the new company’s network (for example, old UNC paths or drive letters). If the root or overrides are not updated, attachments cannot be created or read correctly.

What to do
- For each new company:
  - In FrmAdminGeneral, configure:
    - Root directory for saving attachments (ServerDirectory).
    - Optional overrides for Goods Returns, Customer Support Log, and Customer Support Pool.
  - Test by creating a CSL/GR/CSP entry and verifying folders/files are created at the expected locations and are accessible to users.

3. Email sending

Where configured
- UI: WindowsApplication1\Forms\Admin\FrmAdminGeneral.Designer.vb (grpEmail)
  - txtEmailServerAddy – SMTP host.
  - txtEmailUserName – SMTP login username.
  - txtEmailPassword – SMTP password (encrypted in the DB).
  - txtEmailAddress – Visible From/Reply-To address.
  - txtServerEmailPort – SMTP port.

- Storage: WindowsApplication1\Class\Program Objects\clsProgramSettings.vb
  - Backing fields for UserName, Password, EmailAddress, ServerAddress, Gen_EmailServerPort.

- Logic: WindowsApplication1\Class\ClsEmails.vb
  - Decrypts stored SMTP password using EncryptDecryptVB("PayRise").
  - Builds an SmtpClient with:
    - Host = GenSettings.ServerAddress
    - Port = GenSettings.EmailServerPort
    - EnableSsl = True
    - UseDefaultCredentials = False
    - Credentials from GenSettings.UserName / Password
  - Uses GenSettings.EmailAddress for:
    - From/Reply-To headers
    - Message-Id domain (currently derived from the address domain).

Risks for a new system
- The email-sending code is now fully driven from GenSettings (no hard-coded SMTP host, port, or email domain). The From/Reply-To and Message-Id domain are taken from the configured EmailAddress.
- Remaining risk is configuration only:
  - If new SMTP details (host, port, SSL, credentials) are not entered in FrmAdminGeneral, outbound emails will fail.
  - If the EmailAddress remains set to a Sheppee (or otherwise incorrect) address, the new company will send emails that appear to come from that domain, which is usually wrong and can fail SPF/DMARC checks.

What to do
- Per-company setup in FrmAdminGeneral:
  - Set Email Server Address, Email Port Number, User Name, Password and Address according to the company’s email infrastructure.
  - Confirm with their IT whether SSL should be enabled and which port to use (commonly 587).

4. Reports / URLs

Where
- Code: WindowsApplication1\Modules\ModURL.vb
  - For each report type, pattern is:
    - Read the last row from URL_Lists.
    - Use a specific column (e.g., CSL_By_Customer, End_Of_Year_GR).
    - Launch using Process.Start(webAddress).

- Data: URL_Lists table in the main CSDB database.
  - Contains columns like CSL_Compleated_Last_30_Days, CSL_By_Customer, CSL_By_Date_Range, CSL_Selected_CSL_Report.
  - Goods Returns related: End_Of_Year_GR, Monthly_GR, Monthly_Warrenty_Report, etc.

Risks for a new system
- If URL_Lists retains Sheppee-specific URLs:
  - Links will open reports or dashboards in the wrong environment (if reachable at all).

What to do
- Per new company:
  - Populate URL_Lists with that company’s correct report server URLs.
  - Leave missing reports blank; user will see "No web address linked" where error handling is present.

5. Local machine / runtime dependencies

Where
- WindowsApplication1\App.config – runtime assembly bindings and startup:
  - .NET Framework: v4.0, .NETFramework,Version=v4.8.1.
  - Binding redirects for:
    - Microsoft.ReportViewer.Common
    - System.Buffers
    - System.Memory
    - System.Runtime.CompilerServices.Unsafe
    - K4os.Hash.xxHash, K4os.Compression.LZ4.Streams
    - Google.Protobuf
    - BouncyCastle.Crypto
    - CrystalDecisions.*
    - log4net

Risks for a new system
- Target machines must have:
  - .NET Framework 4.8.1 installed.
  - The Crystal Reports runtime matching the bound versions.
  - The ReportViewer runtime version 11.0.0.0.
- If missing, the application may fail to start or crash when opening reports.

What to do
- When deploying to another company:
  - Install .NET 4.8.1.
  - Install the correct Crystal Reports runtime.
  - Include all required DLLs from the build output.
  - Optionally provide a simple "System Requirements" checklist.

6. Domain-specific naming (non-breaking but company-specific)

Examples
- clsItemMasterData.vb: property SheppeePartNumber (refers to company part numbers, not infrastructure).
- clsGoodsReturns.vb: uses status text "Sheppee to Quote" and font color settings like GR_Sheppee_to_Quote_Font_Colour.
- FrmLinkGRtoCSP.vb: rdoSheppee radio button to select internal warehouse vs external customer.

Risks
- These do not break functionality but may look odd or misleading for other companies.

Suggested action
- If distributing to third parties, consider renaming visible UI text and enum values to more generic names (e.g., Internal Quote, Internal Warehouse) while leaving database schema stable.

7. SQL scripts (tooling only)

Where
- Scripts under the Scripts\ folder such as:
  - Get BOM from SAP.sql
  - Get BOM from SAP Insert.sql
  - Get WOL from SAP2 Insert.sql
  - Copy New Items from SAP to CSDB.sql

Characteristics
- These scripts include hard-coded references to SHEPPEE-SAP-DB and specific database names.
- They are not invoked by the application code at runtime.

Risks
- If someone runs them unmodified in another company’s environment, they will:
  - Fail to connect to the expected servers, or
  - Attempt operations against non-existent or wrong databases.

What to do
- Treat these as Sheppee-internal admin tools.
- For other companies:
  - Either do not ship them, or
  - Provide adapted/parameterized versions with clear documentation.

8. Migration checklist (per new company)

1) Databases
- Create or provision the main CSDB (Customer_Support_DB equivalent) and CSDBLocal helper database on the chosen SQL servers.
- Update all connectionStrings and My.Settings values in App.config to point to the new servers, DBs, and credentials.
- Run the local helper DB creation/seed for CSDBLocal if required.
- Use FrmEnvironmentConnections to configure ConnectionInfo entries for each environment (Environment_CSDB and Environment_SAP).

2) Attachments
- In FrmAdminGeneral, set:
  - Root directory for saving attachments.
  - Optional per-area directories for Goods Returns, Customer Support Log, Customer Support Pool.
- Verify that creating CSL/GR/CSP entries creates folders and files with correct permissions.

3) Email
- In FrmAdminGeneral, configure:
  - SMTP host, port, username, password.
  - From/Reply-To address.
- Coordinate with the new company’s IT to confirm SSL and port settings.

4) Reports
- Populate URL_Lists in the main database with the new company’s reporting URLs.
- Check each report action from the application to ensure URLs open the expected destinations.

5) Local tools and helper DB
- Ensure CSDBLocalConnectionString in App.config correctly identifies where CSDBLocal lives.

6) Runtimes and dependencies
- Install .NET Framework 4.8.1.
- Install the Crystal Reports runtime.
- Include all required assemblies referenced by the application when deploying.

End of Migration Audit.
